***

To fix this vulnerability, you should replace the hardcoded string with a secure method that retrieves the key from the environment variable. This ensures that the key is only available when the application is running, preventing unauthorized access.

```java
// This code demonstrates a secure fix: using System.getenv() to retrieve the API key securely.
// It prevents the key from being exposed in the code or sent over the network.
public class SecureAPIUsage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This is a secure example where the API key is obtained from the environment.
        // It ensures that the key is only accessible within the application context.
        String apiKey = System.getenv("API_KEY"); // Securely retrieves the key
        if (apiKey != null) {
            System.out.println("API Key: " + apiKey);
        } else {
            System.out.println("No API key found in environment.");
        }
    }
}
